WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.650
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Skillshare, home to over 25,000

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:08.730
classes that could teach you a new life skill.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:16.570
On Nov 25th 2018 Dr. He Jiankui announced,
via youtube, that he had decided to push humankind

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.160
across a controversial threshold.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:24.050
This announcement was, of course, that his
work had resulted in the birth of twins who

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.060
had both been subject to genetic engineering
using CRISPR.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.169
This was a threshold widely considered to
be off limits.

00:00:31.169 --> 00:00:37.010
In 2015 two of the foremost journals in research,
Science [1] and Nature [2], both made similar

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.600
statements stating that modification of human
germ cells, that is sperm and egg, should

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:42.870
be avoided.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.090
The scientific community seemed to have come
to an agreement not to experiment with this

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:53.269
technology until we could agree how and when
to apply it to humans, but Dr. He Jiankui

00:00:53.269 --> 00:00:58.820
has unceremoniously dragged us across this
line in the sand, whether we like it or not.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:04.540
Now, more than ever, there is an urgent need
to stop and ask what the outcomes of this

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:09.750
work are for the individuals who have been
subject to it and also how the rest of society

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.000
may be affected.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.000
...

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.140
To understand all this we’re going to need
to understand how CRISPR technology works

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.860
and what the intrinsic shortcomings of this
technology are.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:24.920
DNA is made of nitrogen and carbon based rings
which form 4 distinct chemical codes: Adenine

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.740
(A), Guanine (G), Cytosine (C) and Thymine
(T).

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:33.060
These codes form an easily reproducible double
stranded molecule which is read in group of

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.350
threes, called codons, which are contained
within structures called genes.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.720
These genes are simply the parts of the DNA
that code for the more structurally complex

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:46.170
proteins, which in turn, form the biological
machines and structures that are required

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.390
for all life on earth.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:54.360
Due to DNA’s ability to copy itself, until
now the code in the DNA of all humans can

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.220
be traced faithfully to the first cells on
earth.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:04.070
Dr. He’s work changed all of this by employing
the use of the CRISPR-CAS9 technology to purposefully

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:08.670
change the DNA code in the two twins referred
to as Lula and Nana.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:14.569
As its name suggests, the CRISPR/CAS9 methodology
is dependent on two key parts: CRISPR and

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:15.730
CAS9.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:20.019
CRISPR is a kind of repeating DNA structure
that was first discovered accidentally in

00:02:20.019 --> 00:02:27.290
bacterial DNA by the Japanese scientist Yoshizumi
Ishino in 1987 [3] . For 17 years following

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:32.909
this discovery, the exact function of these
DNA repeats went undiscovered until it was

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:38.500
identified as the means by which bacteria
identify and protect themselves against invading

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.049
viral DNA [4].

00:02:40.049 --> 00:02:45.239
It turned out that these strange DNA repeats
actually came from viruses.

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:50.299
Bacteria have the ability to cut up invading
viral DNA, store it in their own DNA, and

00:02:50.299 --> 00:02:56.359
then subsequently use it as a guide system
for the enzymes CAS9, which destroys the viral

00:02:56.359 --> 00:03:00.010
DNA sequence should it invade again in the
future.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:06.170
In order to do this, CAS9 uses the stored
DNA to make something called a guide RNA.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:12.040
This guide RNA is usually coded for by a CRISPR
repeat, and guides CRISPR to DNA it should

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.040
cut.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.439
There are two conditions for CRISPR’s binding
to its target.

00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:21.920
First Cas9 must bind to the target sequence
and a specific 3 code variation in DNA which

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.439
is called a PAM (protospacer adjacent motif).

00:03:23.439 --> 00:03:28.260
Most PAMs can be recognized by Cas9 as they
contain the code: *GG.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.180
These recurring patterns are targeted by a
bacteria’s Cas9 system as they identify

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.730
the DNA of invading viruses.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.689
This gives the bacteria an ability to target
and destroy the viral DNA.

00:03:40.689 --> 00:03:46.329
However, in the last 5 years, we have learned
to leverage this ability to direct CAS9 with

00:03:46.329 --> 00:03:51.440
guide RNA’s of our own design to alter the
genome of other organisms [5].

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.469
The guide RNA binds to a part of CAS9 called
the alpha-helical lobe.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:04:02.560
In turn, another domain of CAS9, the nuclease
lobe, binds to any flanking part of the DNA

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:08.260
strands that matches with the guide RNA, causing
the double helix structure of the DNA to unwind

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:09.260
[6].

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:14.819
This process is facilitated by the proximity
between the DNA and the Cas9 complex and electrostatic

00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:18.580
interactions that occur between the RNA and
DNA.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:23.170
At this point, 2 specific domains of the nuclease
lobe cut the DNA.

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:27.680
Although we’re not yet fully sure how this
mechanism occurs, comparative studies suggest

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.620
that the most likely way is that the two seperate
parts of Cas9 are able to rip protons from

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:38.240
water molecules, and in turns weaponize the
deprotonated water molecule to break the DNA

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:39.240
apart [ref].

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:44.100
This is done at two sites in the DNA, one
which cleaves the DNA at the target site (HNH)

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.560
and another which cleaves the DNA at a non-target
site (RuvC).

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.610
At this point a number of things can happen
depending on which approach is needed:

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:57.780
- Cas9 can be modified to include a deaminase
enzyme which can target specific bases for

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:00.350
mutation, leading to brand new mutations.

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:06.010
- a process known as homology directed repair
can be used to insert new DNA at the cutting

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:07.010
site.

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:11.710
-Or the targeted piece of DNA can be simply
cut out and removed if it is deemed to be

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:12.710
undesirable.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:18.200
Dr. He Jankui opted for the latter option
and simply cut a section out of a gene called

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.340
CCR5.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:26.360
The section that was cut corresponded to a
mutation known as delta32, that confers it’s

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:28.940
owners with protection to certain strands
of HIV.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:35.060
Dr. He’s data seems to suggest he may have
artificially induced a mutation, similar to,

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:39.550
but not identical to the delta 32 mutation
in these twins [7].

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:45.290
In order to do this, Dr. He had to first optimize
the gene editing protocol in mice, monkey

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:46.730
and human cells.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:51.440
The reason for this is to make sure the protocol
has high specificity - simply put, that the

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.580
gene editing technique was editing the target
DNA and not some other sequence.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:00.880
This claim is of course key to the validity
of the experiment.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.450
Unedited embryos can be thrown away after
all - but for obvious reasons, making unspecific

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:11.810
alterations to a human’s genetic code would
be a major red flag...and Dr. He’s work

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:15.040
raises major red flags for this exact reason.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.710
Their check for specificity of gene editing
used parental genetics as a reference point,

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:25.190
an inadequate approach due to the fact that
a parent’s genetic material is subject to

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:29.750
a certain degree of randomization every time
a sperm or egg is produced.

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:34.570
It’s further complicated by the fact that
no two sperm or eggs will be the same and

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:38.500
that their methodology did not account for
the possibility that larger sections of the

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:44.200
genetic code could be deleted by their implementation
of the Crispr/Cas9 system.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:48.440
Further to this, from the data released so
far we can tell that the mutation induced

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:54.560
in the twins does not exactly mirror the delta32
population that He is attempting to induce

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:55.840
in the twins [8].

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:01.480
Although the protein produced should in theory
largely mirror the naturally occuring mutation,

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:05.330
we cannot tell that for sure because it has
not been tested.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:09.880
Standard practice at this point is to test
these mutations in an animal model in order

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:13.400
to assess unexpected effects of the experiment.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.870
This is perhaps the most troublesome part
of this experiment.

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:22.020
Lulu and Nana are test subjects for a genetic
mutation that we have not even taken the time

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.610
and care to test in animals.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:29.550
Nonetheless, the mutation was induced in a
total of 31 human embryos.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:34.810
These embryos were grown for a period of 5-8
days and the 19 that were deemed “normal”

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:36.430
were taken forward.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:41.500
Out of these 19 embryos 2 were implanted in
the mother to give rise to the twins.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:47.470
These concerns over Lulu’s and Nana’s
well being are only one of a myriad of issues:

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:51.940
Perhaps most critically there was no medical
need for this treatment in the first place.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:57.040
It has been claimed that the biological father
of the twins is HIV positive.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:02.340
Given that the couple used IVF, and that there
is no evidence that HIV would be passed on

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:05.990
from father to child using this method of
conception.

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:08.040
The procedure was medically unnecessary.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:13.020
Furthermore, He claims to have induced this
mutation completely into at least one of the

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:14.020
twins.

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:19.130
To do this, He and his group would have had
to check the DNA in every cell in the growing

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:24.710
blastocyst, a group of cells that exist from
days 5-9 post fertilization that eventually

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:26.450
becomes the embryo.

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:30.780
Given that they would have to destroy the
blastocyst to test all the cells, a clear

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:36.669
logistical problem exists in trying to qualify
that the mutation exists in all the cells.

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:41.690
Accordingly, He and his group only checked
a number of cells from the blastocyst.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:46.319
This means both twins might have a condition
called mosaicism, where the deletion only

00:08:46.319 --> 00:08:48.649
occurs in a subset of their cells.

00:08:48.649 --> 00:08:51.649
This means that different cells in their body,
have different DNA.

00:08:51.649 --> 00:08:56.260
In this context, the effects of this condition
are completely unstudied.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:01.399
Finally, the gene that He took aim at has
also been reported to have a role in learning.

00:09:01.399 --> 00:09:07.009
A link between the activity of the CCR5 gene
product and the dampening down of the brain’s

00:09:07.009 --> 00:09:12.350
ability to form new neural connections has
been previously described in mice models.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:16.649
Theoretically, there is a possibility that
the induced deletion could have a positive

00:09:16.649 --> 00:09:19.360
effect on the twin’s ability to learn.

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:24.310
Right now there are far too many unknowns
to make an accurate prediction on what if

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:29.769
any effect this experiment will have on the
two twins and there’s an even bigger question

00:09:29.769 --> 00:09:34.370
about what effect the announcement will have
on the progress of the field.

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:38.579
Complicating this mess is the fact that this
study did not go in front of an adequate ethics

00:09:38.579 --> 00:09:44.529
approval board and default Chinese law prohibits
any kind of follow up with the twins.

00:09:44.529 --> 00:09:48.589
Because of this, we will likely never know
what will become of Lulu and Nana.

00:09:48.589 --> 00:09:53.990
Dr. He has stated that he plans to publish
the work in a peer reviewed journal, but given

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:58.680
that he has gone M.I.A for almost 2 months
now [9], the scientific community may never

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:03.620
have an opportunity to properly review and
digest the impact of this work.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.620
So where do we go from here?

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:10.860
There is no doubt that genetic engineering
is a valuable tool that can potentially safely

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:13.240
increase human wellbeing and productivity.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:19.029
It’s not unlikely that plunging into these
uncharted waters unaccounted and without approval

00:10:19.029 --> 00:10:24.720
may have a chilling effect on the proper utilization
and development of this technology though.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:29.269
Perhaps, until a consensus on the way forward
is established this is a good thing.

00:10:29.269 --> 00:10:33.730
Thankfully, the World Health Organization
has stepped up to take a moderating role in

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:38.779
the process and are establishing a working
group to establish clear guidelines.

00:10:38.779 --> 00:10:43.100
We need these guidelines because we’ve stepped
into a new era of medicine.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:47.939
One where the genetic modification of humans
is not only a possibility but an increasingly

00:10:47.939 --> 00:10:50.110
real option.

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:53.389
Humans have long sought to improve themselves
through technology.

00:10:53.389 --> 00:10:57.550
From the printing press to the internet, these
inventions exist to expand our knowledge and

00:10:57.550 --> 00:10:58.670
inform us.

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:02.730
Maybe one day we’ll be able to alter our
genes to make ourselves smarter, but in the

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:07.439
meantime we’re already surrounded by tools
for this, like watching educational videos

00:11:07.439 --> 00:11:12.360
on YouTube, or watching in-depth tutorials
and classes like the ones you’ll find on

